From 9372b4d3f62b84f3aebd09163cccb6bfaa69cb7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 09:50:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix chat long-press reliability + WhatsApp-style bulk delete
 sheet
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Root cause of unreliable long-press (esp. on images): our own JS
long-press timer (~450ms) and Android's native contextmenu event for
the same finger-hold both fire close together. The native event was
still routed through openMenu(), which — since selectMode was already
true from our own timer — immediately toggled the just-selected
message back OFF. Fix: openMenu() now bails out when lpFired is
already true for this touch sequence (the oncontextmenu attribute's
'return false' already suppresses the native menu itself; this stops
our own code acting on it a second time).

Also hardens the text-message race: '.msg-bubble/.msg-text' re-enable
native text selection for copy/paste, which competes with our
long-press on the same ~450ms window. Added a 'lp-holding' class,
toggled for the duration of a press on that row only, that disables
native select/callout just long enough to remove the race — restored
immediately if the press doesn't turn into a real long-press.
Also marks message images/gifs draggable=false so a native image-drag
can't hijack the gesture either.

Bulk delete (multi-select toolbar) previously always deleted 'for me'
only, even when every selected message was the user's own — no
'delete for everyone' option existed there at all, unlike the
single-message context menu. Extended the shared confirmDelete()
modal (safe-delete.js) with an optional multi-option mode (stacked
buttons above Cancel) while keeping the original single-button
signature fully backward compatible for every other existing caller.
deleteSelected() now shows 'Delete for everyone' + 'Delete for me'
when every selected message is the user's own (same ownership check
already used for the single-message menu), otherwise only 'Delete for
me' — mirroring the real backend permission (senderRole match) rather
than hard-coding an option it can't perform.
